<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The Holy Spirit is a Person, not a force</h4>
<p>This is the key point. Many catechumens (and many practicing Catholics) have a vague idea of the Holy Spirit as an &laquo;energy&raquo; or a &laquo;force.&raquo; The Catechism is clear: the Holy Spirit is a divine Person, with intelligence and will, who relates to us. Use the wind analogy: you cannot see it, but its effects are real. The Spirit is the same: you cannot see him, but his action in your life is concrete and personal.</p>

<h4>The names of the Spirit</h4>
<p>The Spirit has many names in Scripture: Paraclete, Spirit of truth, Spirit of adoption, Spirit of promise, Spirit of holiness. Each name reveals a facet of his action. Do not try to explain them all; choose the most accessible ones for your group. &laquo;Paraclete&raquo; (= the one who is beside) is usually the one that connects most.</p>

<h4>Gifts vs. fruits</h4>
<p>It is important to distinguish: the gifts are dispositions that the Spirit places in us (they are received, not earned); the fruits are the visible works that spring from those gifts. The analogy: the gifts are the root of the tree; the fruits are what you eat. If there are no fruits, it may be that we are not allowing the gifts to act.</p>

<h4>Pentecost as experience, not just doctrine</h4>
<p>The point is not that catechumens &laquo;know&raquo; what happened at Pentecost, but that they experience it. Invite them to prayer. The Spirit does not impose himself: he is welcomed. If the group is open, a moment of deep silence can be more powerful than any explanation.</p>

<h4>Prayer to the Spirit</h4>
<p>The simplest and most powerful prayer to the Spirit is: &laquo;Come, Holy Spirit.&raquo; No complicated words are needed. Saint John XXIII prayed it constantly. Encourage the catechumens to make this short invocation a daily habit.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the wind analogy: you cannot see it, but it moves leaves, flags, windmills. The Spirit is like that: you cannot see him, but he moves your heart. Activity: make a paper pinwheel and write a gift of the Spirit on each blade. Prayer: &laquo;Come, Holy Spirit, fill my heart like the wind fills the sails of a ship.&raquo;</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience of peer pressure and difficult decisions. The Holy Spirit as the one who gives fortitude to go against the current, counsel to decide well, and interior peace when everything outside is chaos. Use the example of the apostles: before Pentecost they were afraid; afterward, they went out to preach without fear. The difference was the Spirit.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults come from traditions where the Holy Spirit was unknown or confused with emotions. Be clear: it is not &laquo;feeling something&raquo; but the Presence of God acting in us. The gifts are not emotions; they are real dispositions that transform life. Share testimonies of conversions where the Spirit was the protagonist.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the theology of the Spirit: the procession of the Spirit from the Father and the Son (CCC 685-688), the relationship between the Spirit and the Eucharist (epiclesis), the doctrine of charisms (CCC 798-801), and the action of the Spirit in the seven sacraments. You can cite Saint Basil and Saint Augustine on the Spirit as &laquo;gift&raquo; and &laquo;communion.&raquo;</p>

</section>

