<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If the Holy Spirit has dwelt in me since Baptism, why don&rsquo;t I feel him?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Spirit is not &laquo;felt&raquo; the way you feel an emotion. His action is deeper than feelings. Like the wind that moves leaves without being seen, the Spirit works in us in ways we do not always perceive consciously. The fruits (love, peace, joy) are the sign of his presence. If there are fruits, the Spirit is acting, even if you do not &laquo;feel&raquo; him. The key is docility: letting him act, not &laquo;forcing&raquo; his presence (CCC 741-742).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Is the Holy Spirit the same as God&rsquo;s &laquo;energy&raquo; or &laquo;force&raquo;?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No. This confusion is common, especially in contexts where people speak of &laquo;spiritual energy.&raquo; The Holy Spirit is a divine Person, not an impersonal force. He has intelligence (he knows the deep things of God, 1 Cor 2:10-11), will (he distributes gifts &laquo;as he wills,&raquo; 1 Cor 12:11), and the capacity to love (Rom 15:30 speaks of &laquo;the love of the Spirit&raquo;). A force does not love, does not intercede, does not comfort. The Spirit does (CCC 685-686).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Are the gifts of the Spirit the same as charisms?&raquo;</h4>
<p>No, they are different. The gifts of the Holy Spirit (wisdom, understanding, counsel, etc.) are given to all the baptized for personal sanctification. Charisms are special graces that the Spirit distributes &laquo;as he wills&raquo; (1 Cor 12:11) for the good of the Church: healings, tongues, prophecy, etc. The gifts are for the holiness of each person; charisms are for the building up of the community (CCC 798-801).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why did Jesus say it was better for him to go away so that the Spirit would come? Wasn&rsquo;t it better to have Jesus present?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Jesus explained it this way: &laquo;It is to your advantage that I go away, for if I do not go away, the Paraclete will not come to you&raquo; (Jn 16:7). It is not that the Spirit is &laquo;better&raquo; than Jesus, but that Jesus&rsquo; physical presence was limited to one place and time. The Spirit can dwell in each person, in every place, at every moment. The Ascension of Jesus is not a farewell, but an expansion: now Christ is present not only in Palestine, but in every heart that welcomes him (CCC 683-684).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Holy Spirit,<br />
you who filled the Upper Room with wind and fire,<br />
fill my heart before I speak.<br />
Give me wisdom to teach what is true,<br />
counsel to guide what is good,<br />
and fortitude to live what I preach.<br />
May it not be I who speak,<br />
but you who speak through me.<br />
Open the hearts of those who hear me,<br />
that they may know you, love you, and follow you.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

